<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oswestry</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oswestry is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, close to the Welsh border. Its name is linked with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a>, killed nearby at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maserfield">Battle of Maserfield</a> in 642. The town was held at times by English and Welsh rulers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd</a> of Powys built a castle there around 1149, and it later became an English marcher lordship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oswestry.jpg" alt="Oswestry" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Broster · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llwyd_Mansion_1604_Oswestry_(8689969578).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
